<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 10: Looking to the Future  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/ch9-research.md):
  - The four categories come from podcast 077 (RAILect 5). Everything David
    says there is attributed to him. His timings and claims are not verified.
  - Cases already in the book, mapped to the categories (David's own
    examples in 077):
      Never   - AI detectors (Chapter 5)
      When    - Amazon Just Walk Out (Chapter 3)
      If      - AI tutors (this chapter's case study)
      Unknown - Gemini's images (Chapter 7)
  - The case study is the "If" one: AI tutoring. The Nigeria trial rests on
    a World Bank blog (9 Jan 2025). The Kenya part (EIDU) is a J-PAL pilot
    listing with NO results for the AI tool.
  - TODO (Lily): the blog post on the four categories (her PDF).
  - TODO (Lily): categorise the Kenyan cases from earlier chapters
    (Tala, Sama, SHA, the 2026 policy, the moderators, Olkaria). Exercise 6
    and the Try It ask the reader to do it. Lily wants to think about it.
  - TODO: the AI and consciousness podcast (episode id not known).
  - Practise (STACK) section removed on purpose. Questions go in the exercises.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-looking-to-the-future" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Looking to the Future</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-looking-to-the-future">
        <p>
            Every few months there's a new claim about what AI will be able to
            do next.
            Some of them come true quickly.
            Some of them are sold to us for years and never arrive.
            And some of them are still open questions, even for the people
            who build the systems.
        </p>

        <p>
            It would help to have a way of sorting them.
            In this chapter we use one from David Stern, with four boxes:
            what will never be possible, what is a matter of when, what
            depends on an <q>if</q>, and what is still unknown
            (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-077" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 77, 2024</xref>).
            We've already met three of the four in this book.
            Then we take one case, AI tutors, and look at what is known about
            it in Nigeria and Kenya.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe the four categories of future AI developments:
                        the Nevers, the Whens, the Ifs and the Unknowns.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Classify an AI application into one of the four, and
                        justify the choice.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Say what evidence would be needed to move a claim from
                        one category to another.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Think about how assumptions about future AI should shape
                        what we design today, in education and elsewhere.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. The four categories                                         -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-looking-to-the-future-four">
        <title>Four Kinds of Future</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-looking-to-the-future-four">
            <p>
                This is the last of five episodes in a series that David and I
                made for lecturers.
                In it, David sets out the four categories, and gives an example
                for each one.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-looking-to-the-future-podcast-077">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 77: Looking to the Future of AI for Higher Education</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-looking-to-the-future-podcast-077"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/4253/077-looking-to-the-future-of-ai-for-higher-education.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-looking-to-the-future-categories">
            <title>The boxes</title>

            <p>
                David says that however many advances there are, there are
                four different kinds of statement we can make about the future
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-077" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 77, 2024</xref>).
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-looking-to-the-future-four">
                <title>The four categories, in David's words, shortened</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Category</cell>
                        <cell>What it means</cell>
                        <cell>His example</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>The Nevers</cell>
                        <cell>Whatever advances there are, it will make no difference. What we say today stays true.</cell>
                        <cell>Relying on AI to detect AI-written work</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>The Whens</cell>
                        <cell>The tools we have are enough in principle. It's a matter of when, not if.</cell>
                        <cell>Amazon's Just Walk Out shops</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>The Ifs</cell>
                        <cell>It needs a breakthrough, one that he thinks is on the way.</cell>
                        <cell>AI personal tutors</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>The Unknowns</cell>
                        <cell>Nobody yet knows which of the others it belongs in.</cell>
                        <cell>Telling fact from fiction in generated images</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Here's how David explains each one.
            </p>

            <p>
                <em>The Nevers.</em>
                He says that any tool that classifies things will make false
                positives and false negatives, and that you'll never get
                certainty.
                So lecturers should never rely on detection alone.
                He points to the Reading University study that we met in
                Chapter 2, where, as he tells it, only two of the 33 AI-written
                scripts were identified.
                We looked at the problem with detectors in Chapter 5.
                I add in the episode that a writer dismissed an email because it
                used the word <q>delve</q>, which they assumed meant AI, and that
                many people in Nigeria replied that it's an ordinary word for them.
            </p>

            <p>
                <em>The Whens.</em>
                David says that Amazon's shops, which we met in Chapter 3, were
                a hard problem that the current tools could probably solve
                eventually.
                The problem was the scale, with so many products.
                He says that in a much smaller shop, with few products, it would
                probably be solvable now.
                He doesn't say the answer is to keep trying until it works.
                He says he's glad that it isn't being pursued that way.
            </p>

            <p>
                <em>The Ifs.</em>
                David's example is the AI personal tutor.
                He says that AI is good at the style of a tutor, meaning how
                an answer is presented, and weaker on the substance, meaning
                whether the answer is right.
                He says recent advances on mathematics, with systems that did
                well on the maths Olympiad, suggest that
                reliable substance might be coming, but that it needs a real
                advance in technology and not just more of the same.
                We look at this one in the case study below.
            </p>

            <p>
                <em>The Unknowns.</em>
                David's example is Gemini's images, which we met in Chapter 7.
                He says it's not clear to him that it's even a solvable
                problem, to make a system that is diverse where that's right,
                and accurate where history matters.
                It could be a When, or an If, or a Never.
                As he puts it, he doesn't think anyone knows.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    David doesn't claim that he always gets the box right.
                    He says that the discussion about which box something is in
                    is the useful part.
                    It's also something a non-expert can ask an expert: for
                    my idea, which box is it in, and why?
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-looking-to-the-future-design">
            <title>Why bother sorting?</title>

            <p>
                David's answer is that the box tells you how to plan.
                If something is a Never, you can design for that now, and the
                design should still hold in the future.
                If something is an If, you can ask what you would want to be
                ready for if it arrives
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-077" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 77, 2024</xref>).
                For lecturers, he says, that might mean more group work and
                human interaction, because those are the parts that an AI
                tutor can't give.
            </p>

            <!-- TODO (Lily): add the blog post on the four categories here. -->
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Case study: AI tutors                                       -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-looking-to-the-future-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: Can an AI Tutor Teach?</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-looking-to-the-future-promise">
            <title>The promise</title>

            <p>
                Private tutors help students a great deal, and most families
                can't afford one.
                So when chatbots arrived, many people asked the obvious
                question: could every student have one?
                David says that is exactly what Khan Academy hoped for, with
                its tutor Khanmigo
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-077" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 77, 2024</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                A tutor that is patient and available at any hour sounds like a
                good thing, wherever teachers are stretched.
                The question for this chapter isn't whether that's attractive.
                It's which box it belongs in.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-looking-to-the-future-nigeria">
            <title>What happened in Nigeria</title>

            <p>
                The best evidence we found is from Nigeria.
                A World Bank pilot, in Edo in Nigeria, ran for six weeks in June
                and July 2024, with a randomised evaluation.
                The authors say that generative AI, with teacher support, can work
                as a <q>virtual tutor</q>
                (<xref ref="biblio-worldbank-chalkboards-2025" text="custom">De Simone and others, 2025</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The authors report learning improvements of about 0.3 standard
                deviations.
                The pen-and-paper test covered English, AI knowledge and digital
                skills, and the blog doesn't say which part the 0.3 refers to.
                They compare this to <q>nearly two years of typical
                learning</q>, and say that it did better than 80% of the
                studies in a database of trials of education programmes in
                developing countries
                (<xref ref="biblio-worldbank-chalkboards-2025" text="custom">De Simone and others, 2025</xref>).
                They also say that the benefits didn't fade as the programme
                went on, that the programme helped all students and not just the
                strongest, and that girls, who started behind the boys,
                seemed to gain even more.
            </p>

            <p>
                Those are the World Bank authors' claims, in a blog post that
                says the full evaluation was still to be published.
                They are also honest about what they don't know.
                They list open questions about long-term effects, what the role
                of teachers should be, and whether there are any
                <q>negative, undesired effects</q>.
                Attendance was affected by flooding, teacher strikes and
                after-school work.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    Notice what was tested.
                    It was an after-school programme, with teachers, in English.
                    It wasn't a chatbot left alone with a child.
                    The result tells us that AI plus teachers can work.
                    It doesn't tell us that AI instead of teachers can.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-looking-to-the-future-kenya">
            <title>What is happening in Kenya</title>

            <p>
                In Kenya, the closest thing we found is a pilot by EIDU, a
                Kenya-based ed-tech organisation that works with education
                systems on structured teaching for primary and pre-primary
                students.
                J-PAL, a research network, lists a pilot of a generative AI
                tool that EIDU built with Google.org's GenAI Accelerator
                (<xref ref="biblio-jpal-eidu" text="custom">J-PAL, n.d.</xref>).
                It identifies learning gaps, builds student profiles and
                produces tutoring plans of about 15 minutes, for small groups.
            </p>

            <p>
                It's a different idea from a chatbot for each student.
                The page describes the aim as amplifying teacher capacity, and not
                replacing it, but it doesn't say who delivers the small-group
                sessions.
            </p>

            <p>
                What's the evidence?
                J-PAL's page says that preliminary evidence shows EIDU's
                approach improves literacy and numeracy by 0.5 standard
                deviations.
                That sentence is about EIDU's approach to structured teaching,
                and the page doesn't say that it's about the AI tool.
                For the AI tool, the page describes a pilot, and a randomised
                evaluation that is planned for the future
                (<xref ref="biblio-jpal-eidu" text="custom">J-PAL, n.d.</xref>).
                We found no results for it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-looking-to-the-future-which-box">
            <title>Which box?</title>

            <p>
                David puts AI personal tutors in the third box, an If.
                His reason is about substance.
                Without reliable content, he says, a tutor might work really
                well and still not be trustworthy
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-077" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 77, 2024</xref>).
                That is a concern the Nigerian trial doesn't test directly, as far
                as we can tell from the blog, because the programme was run
                with teachers and the post doesn't describe how often the tool
                got things wrong.
            </p>

            <p>
                So here is one way to read the evidence.
                A tutor that works alongside a teacher already seems to be
                worth trying, which looks like a When.
                A tutor that works on its own, in the way the promise suggests,
                depends on advances in reliability, which is David's If.
                Whether it ever will be reliable might even be an Unknown.
                You can disagree with that, and your reasons are what matter.
            </p>

            <p>
                Whichever box you choose, the design question is the one David
                asks.
                If good AI tutors do arrive, what should human teachers be doing
                that adds value on top?
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 3. Illustrative example                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-looking-to-the-future-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Sort the Claims</title>

        <p>
            Find three claims about the future of AI, from the news, from a
            company, or from someone you know.
            For example, <q>AI will replace teachers</q>, <q>AI will diagnose
            every disease</q>, or <q>AI will be able to tell when a student
            has cheated</q>.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Put each one in a box: Never, When, If or Unknown.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Write one sentence on why.
                    What would have to be true for it to be in the box?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What evidence would make you move it to a different box?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Show a friend or a colleague your boxes.
                    Where do you disagree?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 4. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-looking-to-the-future">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-looking-to-the-future-assignment">
            <title>Assignment: a short portfolio</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose one of the four categories of future AI developments: the
                    Nevers, the Whens, the Ifs or the Unknowns.
                    Then write a short portfolio that has two parts.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <em>A. Contextual forecast (250 to 300 words).</em>
                            Pick a future AI development or application that fits your category.
                            Describe it, and say why you place it in this category.
                            Reflect on how it could affect your context, such as your country,
                            sector, school or community.
                            What are the ethical, social or educational implications?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <em>B. Preparedness reflection (150 to 200 words).</em>
                            How can we prepare responsibly for this future, even if it's uncertain?
                            Which principles from this course would help to guide responsible
                            decisions?
                            How can educators or institutions design for flexibility or robustness?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Optional bonus.</em>
                    Add a diagram, a sketch or a short readiness checklist for your context.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-looking-to-the-future-1">
            <title>The four futures</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose one of the four categories: the Nevers, the Whens, the
                    Ifs or the Unknowns.
                    Summarise a case study that you think falls into this
                    category, and say why it fits.
                    What are the implications for education, in your context?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-looking-to-the-future-2">
            <title>Where would you put it?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In this chapter we put AI tutors in the Ifs, though we said
                    that it might be argued differently.
                    Make the case for putting AI tutors in a different box.
                    What would you need to see, in Kenya, to be convinced?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-looking-to-the-future-3">
            <title>Reading the evidence</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Look again at the Nigerian trial and the Kenyan pilot.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What was tested, and what wasn't?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who is telling us the result, and what do they
                            gain from it?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Which questions would you want answered before you
                            gave an AI tutor to every school in a county?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-looking-to-the-future-4">
            <title>Design for the future</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David says that if good AI tutors become available, the
                    value of human teaching might be in group work and human
                    interaction.
                    Pick a subject and a level that you know.
                    How would you design the course so that it's still worth
                    doing if every student had a good AI tutor?
                    How would you design it if detection is a Never?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-looking-to-the-future-5">
            <title>Sort the Kenyan cases</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <!-- TODO (Lily): worked through yourself, then give a model answer. -->
                    Think back through the Kenyan cases in this book: Tala's
                    lending, Sama's data workers, SHA's means test, Kenya's
                    2026 policy, the moderators' cases and the Olkaria data
                    centre.
                    For each one, ask what the next step would be if things
                    went well, and what would need to be true.
                    Which box would you put the <em>next step</em> in?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-looking-to-the-future-6">
            <title>Could AI ever be conscious?</title>
            <statement>
                <!-- TODO: add the podcast on AI and consciousness once we have its id. -->
                <p>
                    Listen to the podcast on AI and consciousness, in which
                    David and I look at what we mean by consciousness, and
                    whether AI systems could ever be truly conscious.
                    Then think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Do you think AI could ever be conscious?
                            Why or why not?
                            Which box would you put it in?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What are the risks if we assume consciousness where
                            there is none?
                            What about the reverse?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How does thinking of AI as <q>intelligent but not
                            conscious</q> change how we should design or
                            regulate it?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Should ethical rights or responsibilities ever be
                            extended to AI systems?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

